 the color."}]}]},{"name":"opacity","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"An optional degree of opacity, given in the range "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0"},{"type":"text","text":" to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"1"},{"type":"text","text":". A value of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0"},{"type":"text","text":" means 100% transparency, while a value of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"1"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"means 100% opacity. The default is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"1"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"This initializer creates a constant color that doesn’t change based"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"on context. For example, it doesn’t have distinct light and dark"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"appearances, unlike various system-defined colors, or a color that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"you load from an Asset Catalog with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"init(_:bundle:)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A standard sRGB color space clamps each color component — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"red"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"green"},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"blue"},{"type":"text","text":" — to a range of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"1"},{"type":"text","text":", but OpenSwiftUI colors"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"use an extended sRGB color space, so"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"you can use component values outside that range. This makes it"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"possible to create colors using